/** YD001 quality-pass translations siman 104 */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `Field mouse. Shach wrote: it appears from the Mechaber's words that a field mouse certainly imparts beneficial taste in beer and vinegar; and it is possible that even in wine, oil, and the like it does not spoil. But in the Gemara it is proven that even though it is served on kings' tables, we are uncertain whether it is noten ta'am lifgam in mixtures.`,
    '1#ב': `Issurim. Beit David wrote: the reason is that with a sheretz we are concerned lest something the size of a lentil remain, which is the measure of its tumah and it is a beriah and is not nullified; and Taz challenged him at length and refuted his words, concluding that in truth there is no distinction between sheretz and other issurim. What is forbidden when cut into pieces is not because of beriah importance but because we fear a thin piece was absorbed in the beer or vinegar; this deals with a recognizable issur that cannot be clarified — in that case sixty does not help, for sixty helps only when the issur is not recognizable. So too for other issurim; and so is the view of Rashal and Bach (see Kenesset HaGedolah who refutes Taz; see Peri Chadash who expanded).`,
    '2#א': `Spoils. Rambam gave a reason that these things must be spiced (and Peri Chadash wrote: we have only the ruling of Shulchan Aruch, even in fat, see there). Rashal wrote: one should be stringent in all liquids except wine, oil, and honey.`,
    '2#ב': `For a safek. Shach wrote: this is not a double safek — there is also a safek whether it imparts beneficial taste or spoils; and one must say this safek is not called a safek since the Gemara itself was uncertain; or perhaps it is like safek due to lack of knowledge, and requires study.`,
    '2#ג': `There is. The reason is in Shach: whatever is poured and touches the mouse becomes forbidden and mixes with the rest of the fat and all becomes forbidden; and even though sometimes the mouse lies at the bottom of the bowl, one might say we should permit all fat above the layer on the mouse — one can answer that when they pour boiling fat on it everything melts, so the pour always touches the mouse and all becomes forbidden.`,
    '2#ד': `Pickling. Taz wrote: but if we know for certain the fat was initially soft and there is a safek that it hardened during the pickling period — i.e., within twenty-four hours from falling — we are stringent, unlike Or Zarua who permits if it came before us hard, for we follow the time of finding; and even if it was certainly soft at the time of issur, perhaps it hardened within twenty-four hours of the mouse falling; he brought proof from "all tumah follows the time of finding." Beit David disagrees: with one safek we are stringent. Shach: if it is known it was soft when the mouse fell in, we forbid from safek that it was pickled a full day even if now hard, unless we know clearly it was not soft a full day after the mouse entered; and if it was known soft a full day and later hardened and we do not know if the mouse was there while soft, it is forbidden; however in all this it is permitted if there is sixty, unlike when boiling fat was poured on it.`,
    '2#ה': `Loss. Taz wrote: this refers to the beginning of the hagahah "some are stringent," meaning where there is great loss one may be lenient in all liquids except beer and vinegar. What Rama wrote about fat that is repulsive — meaning it is forbidden because there is no sixty; it seems if there is sixty it is permitted even for lighting; Or Zarua similarly: if repulsive to him, even with sixty or a hundred it is forbidden.`,
    '3#_': `Majority. Shach wrote: even if there is a kezayit within the time of eating a half-loaf it is permitted; see siman 107 and siman 84 on worms — they are not called repulsive things like flies and mosquitoes; but Maharshal and Bach forbid flies and mosquitoes too; however the custom is to be lenient, and Bach permits with great loss.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#א': `Conclusion of the Gemara Avodah Zarah daf 68.`,
    '1#ב': `There in the incident of Rav, who forbade it; and so Rif, Rosh there, and Rambam chapter 16 of Forbidden Foods.`,
    '1#ג': `Rosh there.`,
    '1#ד': `There in the name of Ritzba.`,
    '1#ה': `There in chapter 7 of Chullin; and Ran there in the name of a Gaon.`,
    '1#ו': `Beit Yosef from Tur's words: a sheretz whose measure is like a lentil to be liable on it; and even less than a lentil there is issur; and in my humble opinion I saw he wrote there: specifically in liquid because it can be strained — it is like something that has a way to permit; but if lost in thick food and not recognizable, even a kezayit of the eight sheratzim is well nullified after it is not whole; and I saw Taz expanded on this in his book and explained Tur and Beit Yosef so they do not contradict those who wrote in the name of Or Zarua — see there.`,
    '2#_': `Tur in the name of Rambam chapter 15 (and Rashba and Ran in the name of our French rabbis).`,
    '3#_': `There in the name of Rashba; and see siman 66.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `(Lekut) Field mouse, etc. These are the Mechaber's words and they are surprising, for from what he attacks it is to permit (because of noten ta'am lifgam) according to the opinion (that Rav's reason) is because it is a novelty; and no posek distinguished in this at all (end of matter).`,
    '1#ב': `(Lekut) We are uncertain. Like Rava there, for the conclusion is like him and not like Rav Sheshet (end of matter).`,
    '1#ג': `And therefore, etc. There: and the halachah is both this and that, etc.`,
    '1#ד': `If not, etc. For cold in cold is permitted as stated in chapter Keitzad (76a) and other places; and Tosafot in Avodah Zarah there 68b s.v. "that one," etc.`,
    '1#ה': `Twenty-four hours. Orach Chaim siman 105.`,
    '1#ו': `Whether it was cut, etc.; and if cut, etc.; and specifically, etc. Rashi 69a s.v. "crumbled," etc.; and as stated in Me'ilah 16b; Tosafot and Ran challenged why this was needed — we hold half a measure is forbidden d'oraisa; and furthermore, strain it in a sieve and it should be permitted. Shulchan Aruch says: and if cut, etc., and it is in a case, etc. — this answers Ran's second question; and even with sixty, for the Gemara means it does not forbid at all because of noten ta'am lifgam; nevertheless they forbade if cut. On Ran's first question, the hagahah says: and specifically, etc., as Rashal and Shach wrote — the bones themselves are not nullified even in a majority while standing alone, called dry in dry; rather we come because of beriah; and even a crushed beriah is nullified here since its measure is a lentil it is like a beriah. They were challenged by Taz and Peri Chadash: even if very large, as long as it is not whole it is not called beriah; only the issur is not nullified while recognizable, as Yerushalmi chapter 2 of Orlah; and if the heter is recognizable, one does not nullify the issur, as Terumot chapter 4 — how fifty figs, etc.; therefore nullification is only wet in wet or dry in dry. Shulchan Aruch: whether it melted completely, etc. — wet in wet; and if cut, etc. — dry in wet, meaning in thick food like thick gravy, still wet since not standing alone, and even in a thousand not nullified since by touch in the dish one senses the issur — recognizable issur [but Rashi's view as Tosafot Zevachim 72a s.v. "and let them be nullified," etc. — whatever is forbidden in less than a kezayit is beriah at its issur measure like gid; no question from ant since specifically separated, etc., and it is like a whole ant; Rashi is correct; and Tosafot there, even though in Makot they did not write so but like Rosh, Tosafot in Zevachim are more correct; and one can say Rashi: here a limb is forbidden even in a thousand; and Tosafot in Me'ilah]. (Lekut) And specifically, etc. From Rashi there s.v. "crumbled," etc.; and so in Yevamot 114b s.v. "in something," etc.; though Tosafot there s.v. "because," etc. challenged — Tosafot are difficult, for in Me'ilah the opposite is said; perhaps Tosafot's question is because the verse says "goes on its belly," etc., and "you shall not eat them" to warn, and these are not among the separated ones (end of matter).`,
    '2#א': `If it fell, etc. From what they were uncertain — from this it is clear that other liquids are obviously permitted.`,
    '2#ב': `And some, etc. So from responsum of Rosh; and possibly he holds the same for other liquids; and so from Rambam who wrote: if for wine and oil it is permitted because these must be spiced — implying other liquids are forbidden; and the question in vinegar is to exclude wine that is permitted.`,
    '2#ג': `Even, etc. For whatever is poured and touches the mouse becomes forbidden and is like issur that fell into two pots, each forbids the other; and the poured fat mixes again with the rest and all becomes forbidden. Beit David.`,
    '2#ד': `And where the fat, etc. Meaning: even if permitted to eat as stated in chapter 6 of Bava Batra (97b) and other places.`,
    '3#א': `Repulsive things, etc. We learn from the mouse; and as the Gemara says there, not like Rav Sheshet who said it is a novelty, etc.; therefore whatever the heter is greater it is permitted, for the substance is nullified in the majority; and see siman 103 seif 2; and if its substance is not in the mixture it is always permitted as there; and so at the end of Gittin: "you have a person for whom a fly," etc. — he sucks it and is not called wicked except because of "do not abominate yourselves," and he is not an istenis; with this even Ran who disagrees in siman 103 seif 2 agrees that here, where they are completely spoiled and repulsive, it does not help at all that one's soul is disgusted by them.`,
    '3#ב': `However, etc. So too Ran, for we learn from there.`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(1) [Seif 1] Field mouse imparts beneficial taste, etc. — it appears from the Mechaber that a field mouse certainly imparts beneficial taste in beer and vinegar since it is served on kings' tables; and possibly therefore even in wine, oil, and the like it does not spoil. Shach s.k. 1. Bach similarly: for Shulchan Aruch even wine, oil, and honey it forbids. But Peri Chadash section 1 wrote it is possible that even for Shulchan Aruch a field mouse spoils wine, oil, or honey — unlike Bach and Shach; but in beer and vinegar it certainly imparts beneficial taste — see there. Peri Toar end of section 1 wrote: in anything that does not completely spoil, one should be concerned with a field mouse for safek d'oraisa; one may not permit a field mouse except by tasting a sample — see there. Therefore for us, who do not rely on sampling as above siman 98 section 2, one may estimate sixty; so too Shafan David section 1. However Minchat Chinuch on Tur HaChadash general rule 22 section 25 wrote: field mouse in other liquids one may permit with great loss — to me it seems one should not be lenient even with great loss — see there. So too Arukh HaShulchan section 7: field mouse is like all issurim and forbids in everything until sixty — see there.`,
    '2#_': `(2) There. If it did not remain inside twenty-four hours it is permitted. And what is written below siman 105 seif 1 — if it remained long enough to boil on fire, etc. — requires study in our words there in siman.`,
    '3#_': `(3) There. And no substance of it remained — it is permitted by sixty in the heter against the mouse, etc. And if repulsive to him, even if not repulsive to others it is forbidden to him even with sixty or a hundred because of "do not abominate yourselves." Or Zarua general rule 32 law 26. So too in Sefer Api Ravrevi daf 57a. Shach s.k. 8. Kenesset HaGedolah in Hagahat Beit Yosef end of siman. Peri Chadash end of section 9. Minchat Chinuch on Tur HaChadash general rule 22 end of section 24. Lechem HaPanim section 3. Beit Levi section 3. Chokhmat Adam section 8. Chayei David siman 1 daf 30 end side 2. And see above siman 84 section 29 in the name of several poskim — see there.`,
  },
  'kereti': {
    '1#א': `Beneficial taste. Shach challenged: whence do we know noten ta'am lifgam for Shulchan Aruch to say field mouse, etc.; see Peleti to reconcile Shulchan Aruch's words.`,
    '1#ב': `And specifically in sheretz — see Peleti for Beit David: dry in dry, not its kind, kezayit within the time of eating a half-loaf; and also whether sheretz the size of a lentil is called beriah — see there.`,
    '2#א': `On it. Even for those who do not say a piece becomes nevelah, when boiling soft fat was poured on it, it can be squeezed and is forbidden; and when it mixed again the mixture is forbidden; and this is not difficult with "what became forbidden through absorption does not forbid mixtures" — see what I wrote in Peleti.`,
    '2#ב': `Even, etc. See Peleti where I expanded to permit with loss if not known it was soft when the mouse fell and the fat is hard — for presumption favors the matter that fat fell in; how could it fall in while hard? See Peleti.`,
    '2#ג': `In Nekudot HaKesef: the fat was hot, therefore it requires twenty-four hours, not because of pickling — for that would be double safek.`,
    '2#ד': `Repulsive. And even if there is sixty it does not help — Peri Chadash.`,
    '3#_': `If the heter is greater — even though the issur is within the time of eating a half-loaf, since there is no taste it is like its kind in its kind — Peri Chadash.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Shulchan Aruch siman 104 seif 1) Mouse in beer, etc. Shach s.k. 1: it appears from the Mechaber that field mouse, etc., is obvious that it imparts beneficial taste in beer, etc.; but in the Gemara it is proven that even though it is served on kings' tables we are uncertain whether noten ta'am lifgam in a mixture — see there. At first glance it is incomprehensible: the Gemara says a mouse fell into a barrel of beer and Rav forbade that beer; the rabbis told R' Shimon; shall we say Rav holds noten ta'am lifgam is forbidden? See there. Rava said: the halachah is noten ta'am lifgam is permitted, and a mouse in beer — we do not know why Rav forbade. If because Rav holds noten ta'am lifgam is forbidden and we do not rule like him; if because Rav holds noten ta'am lifgam is permitted and a mouse in beer imparts beneficial taste — then it seems obvious the sugya is a city mouse, and we are uncertain whether it imparts beneficial taste or spoils; but for a field mouse we are not uncertain at all. One can say Shach explains the sugya: certainly for a city mouse we are not uncertain whether it spoils, but we are uncertain for a field mouse; and "this in the field, this in the city" means: since R' Shimon generally holds noten ta'am lifgam is permitted, here it is a novelty; they challenge: it is served on kings' tables — if so there is no novelty; if field mouse in beer is noten ta'am lifgam, why forbid? Therefore: since there is a mouse that is repulsive and nevertheless the Torah forbade, noten ta'am lifgam is forbidden for a mouse; so a field mouse in beer, even if noten ta'am lifgam, is forbidden — clear.`,
    '2#_': `(There in hagahah) And specifically in sheretz there is concern, etc.; but in other issurim there is no concern. See Shach s.k. 3 and what he wrote in the name of Ran on Rashi: why was this needed — in other issurim too there is concern at least for less than a kezayit, for we hold half a measure is forbidden d'oraisa — see there. It seems Tosafot's question: kezayit within the time of eating a half-loaf — we learn that half a measure is forbidden d'oraisa; and "half a measure" is forbidden only b'ein, but through mixture it is permitted; but kezayit within the time of eating a half-loaf is forbidden even through mixture; if so when mixed in more than the time of eating a half-loaf one cannot say half a measure; and this is only when the issur measure is a kezayit; but if the issur measure is very small like sheretz the size of a lentil, through mixture one may forbid since mixture does not negate to nullify; therefore one can distinguish sheretz from other issurim; and Ran disagrees with Rashi — clear.`,
    '3#_': `(There seif 2 in hagahah) And some are stringent in fat, etc.: if they poured daily fat in the pot and perhaps the mouse was there when they heated boiling fat — all forbidden. Even though we hold what became forbidden through absorption does not forbid mixtures as above siman 98 — one must say we distinguish lest all the fat is layers and not because of absorption.`,
  },
  'mechaber': {
    '1#main': `The law of a mouse found in wine or beer. In it are 3 seifim: A field mouse imparts beneficial taste, for it is served on kings' tables; but a city mouse — we are uncertain whether it imparts beneficial taste in beer and vinegar or whether it spoils; and therefore if it fell into beer or vinegar while cold and they removed it whole, if it did not remain inside twenty-four hours it is permitted; but if it was boiling, or even cold and remained inside twenty-four hours — whether they removed it whole, whether it was cut into fine pieces and one can strain it in a sieve so nothing remains, whether it dissolved completely and became entirely liquid and no substance remained — it is permitted by having sixty in the heter against the mouse; and if it was cut into fine pieces and cannot be strained, such as when the beer or vinegar was mixed into thick food — all is forbidden and there is no nullification, for we fear one may encounter the substance of the issur and not notice: {Rama: and specifically for sheretz we are concerned if something remains that cannot be removed; but for other issurim there is no concern (so it appears in Arukh general rule 32).}`,
    '2#main': `If it fell into wine, oil, or other liquids — it certainly spoils and one does not need sixty to nullify its emission: {Rama: and accordingly if it fell into fat the law is the same; and some are stringent in fat, even if hard before us: if they pour fat daily into the pot, there is room to suspect the mouse was there when they heated boiling fat on it — all is forbidden, even if there is sixty in the fat against the whole mouse; and if they did not heat on it, or heated and it is known the mouse was not there when they heated and the fat came before us hard (responsum of Rosh, Hagahot Maimonides chapter 15, Hagahot Mordechai on Chullin, Hagahat Sh"D, and Arukh) — and likewise if the mouse is found on it, removal of place suffices and we do not establish issur lest the fat was soft when it fell there, for pickling is like cooking and permitted from double safek: safek it fell when hard; and if you say when soft, perhaps it hardened before pickling. And where the fat is repulsive to eat, it is forbidden to light it in synagogue because of "bring it to your fire" (Ran in the name of a Gaon, Rashba Pesachim, R' Yerucham, and Or Zarua in the name of Mordechai Pesachim, and the author in Orach Chaim siman 124 seif 12). And where there is great loss one may rely on the lenient words.}`,
    '3#main': `Repulsive things at which a person's soul is disgusted, such as ants, flies, and mosquitoes that every person separates from because of their repulsiveness — even if they were mixed into a dish and their bodies were crushed into it, if the heter is greater they are permitted; and nevertheless, wherever one can check and pass through a sieve, one checks and strains (however in vinegar and beer there is concern like with a mouse) (Beit Yosef according to Rashba).`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Shulchan Aruch siman 104, Taz s.k. 1) He expanded to challenge Rama and his supporters; all his words are surprising. At the root of his foundation he wondered: in the laws of beriah we said in siman 100 regarding an ant that if part was crushed the importance is nullified, etc. — great wonder at his wonder, for Rama and his supporters' reason is that a mouse is a sheretz and its tumah is like a lentil and one is liable for eating it like a lentil; this does not apply to an ant, for it is simple in Mishnah and Gemara and poskim that only the eight sheratzim, including the mouse, have measure like a lentil, but not other sheratzim; and in any case there is no question from ant to mouse according to what is written below that even like a lentil from a mouse is nullified dry in dry; and what he wrote is also written in Or Zarua general rule 25 law 2 — like a lentil of the eight sheratzim, and a crushed sheretz, etc., even though one is liable on them, they are nullified — he was not precise, for Or Zarua deals explicitly with dry in dry, and in that certainly Rama agrees it is nullified dry in dry; and Rama's intent is not that like a lentil from sheretz is not nullified dry in dry because of beriah, but here the law is wet in wet since it mixed in liquid; but if so why forbidden — it should be nullified with sixty against it; rather certainly this case is different since it is like beriah, for one is liable d'oraisa on like a lentil, therefore the rabbis were more stringent and we fear lest one encounter the substance of like a lentil and come to d'oraisa liability as where they decreed on beriah that is not nullified; but in other issurim, since one is liable only on a kezayit, they did not decree; and so is explicit in Or Zarua general rule 32: if it crumbled, throw away the mouse and the taste is nullified in sixty like any taste of beriah; but if it crumbled and was cut in a way one cannot clarify and remove the issur, it is forbidden here lest a piece like a lentil remained and it is beriah; specifically in liquid; but if lost until unrecognizable, even a kezayit of the eight sheratzim is well nullified like other issurim in sixty after it is not whole, as stated above general rule 25 — end of his words; clearly they distinguish: only in liquid they fear lest a piece like a lentil remained and it is beriah; but dry in dry it is well nullified in sixty; and this is why poskim did not write the law of fearing lest one encounter issur substance except here with a mouse; and Rashi, Ra'avan, Mordechai, and Agudah all wrote here the reason: since sheretz's measure is like a lentil, etc., intending like Rama; and so is clear in Shach s.k. 3; and so Riaz in Shiltot Avodah Zarah daf 82 end 1: the mouse rotted and crumbled in liquids — these are forbidden even in more than sixty lest like a lentil from the sheretz enter the liquids and sheretz's measure is like a lentil — end; understand specifically for mouse we fear this, but not other issurim; and as Shach wrote there — clear proof from Rashba himself regarding a gnat that fell into a pot of soup: if a limb was cut from it, it is nullified; and so is proven in Shulchan Aruch siman 100 seif 3: a pot of soup into which a beriah fell and was lost — all is forbidden; implying specifically a whole beriah; if part was cut, it is permitted and we do not fear encountering issur substance — we certainly do not fear this except for mouse, one of the eight sheratzim, as I wrote. What he wrote is also proven from Tosafot who challenged: how do we eat bee honey when bee legs are mixed in the honey, etc. — nothing is proven, for Tosafot there challenge that our eyes see bee legs mixed in honey and we eat honey with bee legs; and furthermore bee legs are whole limbs, meaning like donkey bones; and what we say limbs have no measure, even less than a kezayit from a corpse and less than a lentil from sheretz — is for tumah and when there is flesh on them; otherwise they are pure; and for eating it is fine — end; with this his challenge from their words that they concluded "what we say limbs have no measure," etc., is rejected. And what he wrote is also proven from their words there that they concluded: and what we say limbs have no measure, even less than a kezayit from a corpse and less than a lentil from sheretz, etc. — learn that it deals with less than a lentil — he was not precise, for specifically for limbs there is no measure, and even less than a lentil — otherwise a lentil is required; and all this is clear.`,
  },
  'peleti': {
    '1#א': `Field mouse, etc. Shach wondered: whence for Shulchan Aruch to say field mouse certainly imparts beneficial taste in beer and vinegar — in the Gemara we only say that since it is served on kings' tables it is not repulsive; but what does that have to do with noten ta'am lifgam? One can say the Beit Yosef wondered why the Gemara brought this pilpul: whether field mouse is repulsive or served on kings' tables — for the law there is no difference, for a mouse itself is forbidden, and for emission too there is no difference, for even city mouse if it fell where it imparts beneficial taste it is forbidden, and in the field likewise one eats taste whether it imparts beneficial taste or spoils; therefore we are uncertain whether it imparts beneficial taste or spoils in itself — if in substance it spoils, one can say its emission's taste also spoils; this is the safek in the Gemara whether it imparts beneficial taste; if like its substance so its emission, then field mouse that in substance is praised, its taste too has no safek that it is highly praised; therefore the Gemara brought it and his words are correct.`,
    '1#ב': `And if it was cut, etc. See acharonim who expanded and investigated; the essence is Shach and Kenesset HaGedolah: certainly it is not beriah as Or Zarua wrote explicitly that sheretz like a lentil is stricter than kezayit of nevelah that is not beriah; however the reason seems as Shach and Kenesset HaGedolah wrote: those fine pieces stand alone and cannot be removed because of the thickness of the beer, so it is dry in dry. According to what I raised below siman 109 in the words of Beit David and Maharshal: dry in dry, not its kind, kezayit within the time of eating a half-loaf — the reason: if one ate them all he would come to lashes, for an olive of issur mixed in six olives; but if mixed in more, one cannot come to lashes — if one ate a hundred olives there is still no clear olive of issur; and if one eats more he already exceeds the time of eating a half-loaf and there is no combination for lashes; if so sheretz in food the size of a kezayit — lashes measure is like a lentil even if mixed in a thousand olives, if one ate them all he would come to lashes since within the time of eating a hundred olives one would eat a lentil of sheretz by itself and there would be lashes liability; what difference to me nevelah olive within six olives that within eating a half-loaf one reaches lashes liability, and what difference sheretz in a hundred that within eating a half-loaf one reaches lashes liability — therefore certainly it is not nullified dry in not its kind; if so. These matters are clear to that approach; what to investigate for that approach see below siman 109; but here the law is true law for that approach without doubt. And regarding whether it is beriah: know Rambam and Rashba disagree on the law of separated limbs of sheratzim that impart tumah, the eight sheratzim in the Torah; Rambam chapter 2 of Forbidden Foods: after their death, one who eats a limb from them nevertheless its measure is like a lentil like eating from sheretz; there is no distinction between in their quantity or limbs; and so seems Tosafot Avodah Zarah regarding bee legs; therefore a limb from them is not called beriah, for it needs measure like sheretz; one cannot say "do not eat" between large and small. But Rashba Avodah Zarah 104b wrote explicitly: the eight sheratzim that impart tumah with tumah of a lentil — their eating for lashes is also like a lentil; and so the law for their limbs that impart tumah in any amount — their eating prohibition is likewise, etc. — he holds one is liable for eating in any amount; if so this is included in "you shall not eat of them" large or small, since there is no measure for eating; and if one divides, its name "whole limb" no longer applies, and this is a full beriah like a limb from the living; on the contrary, for living limb at least a kezayit is required, only a majority of flesh suffices; here no measure at all. However Rashba wrote explicitly it is not called beriah for not being nullified; and possibly even a limb from the living Rashba did not count among beriah that is not nullified, neither in Terumah nor in his novellae; and possibly also not called forbidden from the beginning of its creation, for in life we require measure as explained in the Gemara; there in Avodah Zarah (though one can question: this issur from not eating sheretz was in life too, only for lashes there was no measure — nevertheless issur was immediate; it is not like nevelah of impure fowl whose nevelah issur did not apply from life) — therefore for this reason Rashba did not count it beriah; at least for Rosh who does not require forbidden from the beginning of creation, certainly it is beriah; if so in this mouse that dissolved, certainly one tiny whole limb has beriah law and is not nullified in a thousand; and truly Rashi's words that eating is like its tumah like a lentil do not teach this — one can push that it is likewise for limbs that have no measure; and Rashi cited eating and tumah as one law, and requires study.`,
    '2#א': `All forbidden — each time they heated it becomes forbidden like absorption, and Peri Chadash wrote: this is for those who hold piece becomes nevelah; but for those who do not hold piece becomes nevelah, if there is now sixty against all it is permitted; and truly difficult per his words, for this law is also in Or Zarua and he holds without noda b'hutrah piece becomes nevelah; and here it was not noda b'hutrah — one must say here it is not wet in wet, it does not blend well, it immediately congeals; if so Or Zarua wrote and Tosafot Bekhorot as I wrote above: even if permitted, it can be squeezed and is forbidden; so too for those who do not hold piece becomes nevelah, nevertheless it can be squeezed and is forbidden — if so. And so is proven that this law is in responsum of Rosh, and Rosh holds we do not say piece becomes nevelah in all issurim; if so contradictory — unless what is forbidden through absorption does not need sixty to nullify, so what if it became forbidden like absorption — nevertheless it is nullified and sixty is not needed; strained to say the absorption layer grew so much daily until majority — Rabbenu Tam and R' Yehudah who disagree with R' Abba hold this is strained; therefore it seems as I wrote: forbidden piece agrees and they forbade only in ben yomo as I wrote.`,
    '2#ב': `Permitted from double safek, etc. Beit David general rule 32 law 3 wrote: if known the fat was soft when the mouse fell, we forbid from safek perhaps it was pickled in it a full day even if now hard before us, unless known clearly it was not soft a full day after the mouse entered; and if known it was soft a full day and later hardened and we do not know if the mouse was in it while soft, all is forbidden — d'oraisa safek like Rosh and Mordechai if safek it was there when boiling fat was heated, unlike Or Zarua who permitted in both — see there, brought by Shach. I wonder: why not establish the fat on presumption of heter and now a moment before it hardened — from the Torah we establish merit; what Tosafot wrote at the beginning of Niddah: this is for kodshim, but for hullin all is permitted — we say "now it happened"; they wrote explicitly regarding a box they used and later a sheretz was found — we do not establish impurity presumption for taharot used earlier, for hullin all permit and Chizkiyah and R' Yochanan disagreed only for kodshim — see there beginning of Niddah; if so why establish impurity presumption retroactively? Truly for Rashba regarding an animal found tereifah, if the wound scabbed and it was certainly three days before, we establish issur retroactively: whatever you cannot say happened now and must say happened earlier, presumption already failed — if so here too: now it hardened and one cannot say it fell into the fat now and must say retroactively — perhaps it was pickled a full day before; but per Rama who ruled above siman 71 that even with scabbed wound, cheeses made before are permitted — we establish on presumption and say "now it happened" and it became tereifah; why not say so here? I searched and found no distinction in any way. What Rama brought as proof from tumah following time of finding — one can answer when we do not know it was soft twenty-four hours before, we say as now hard before us so it was before; but when we know it was soft twenty-four hours before and only do not know if the mouse was initially inside — on the contrary we establish from time to time and say: here it is found, here it was; but I already wrote this is all for kodshim; see Niddah 4a s.v. tevel, etc.; and wonder at Or Zarua who himself rules cheeses like above and wrote not like mikveh "lacking and came" — so too here; requires study.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `In sheretz. See Sefer Tiferet LaMoshe who wrote: specifically the eight impure sheratzim; but other sheratzim — no; and what is called "frog" is not among the eight sheratzim — see there; and so wrote in responsum Chavat Yair end of siman 102 — see there; and see in responsum Achaz Panim Me'irrot volume 1 siman 28.`,
    '2#_': `To wine and oil. See in responsum Radbaz volume 2 siman 917 who wrote: all the more so it spoils in a dish — see there further.`,
    '3#_': `[On "into the pot and there is safek." See in responsum Chatam Sofer siman 94 who wrote to a rabbi: why establish on presumption of life — I do not know what is wrong that it was alive; even if it remained inside the time for emission while alive or dead, sheretz always forbids; and what Peri Megadim Orach Chaim siman 497 wrote: in live fish swallowing does not apply — he means they swallowed chametz or milk and the reason is that through heat nature turned to meat and blood; I know an incident: a chicken flew into boiling butter on the fire, died inside, and a gaon ruled the butter forbidden even for a non-Jew and they poured it in the streets; and they mentioned again that fowl in milk is permitted b'hanaah and he answered: so too b'hanaah is permitted and to sell to a non-Jew is forbidden because of absorption of a Jew that is forbidden to a non-Jew; and wrote there that Hashem prepared an answer to save from embarrassment since initially he did not intend this; and from Peri Megadim siman 98 s.k. 1 it seems he too did not lean on this; from this we learn emission applies even while alive; and clear proof from Rosh's words in the sugya of mouse in beer — see there. What he further challenged: let us establish now that it fell — end there see. However in Sefer Ikkei Dinim siman 7 section 22 he brought in the name of Teshurat Chen siman 80: if a live mouse or impure thing fell, etc., we do not count twenty-four hours, only from death, for while alive even if it remained many days it does not forbid — see there; and so is the leaning opinion; and the challenge and proof above must be resolved; and see Taz siman 497 and responsa mentioned in Peri Chadash there s.k. 1 — requires study]:`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Shulchan Aruch siman 104 seif 2 in hagahah) Removal of place suffices. That we require removal is a mere stringency because it is repulsive, or because goose fat's way is not to be so hard. Minchat Chinuch.`,
    '2#_': `(There) And where there is great loss one may rely. But without great loss one should be stringent in fat — one can say it is like beer and vinegar; but in other liquids the ruling is to be lenient — so is Rama's view explained in Beit David — see there.`,
    '3#_': `(Taz s.k. 7) He ruled to be stringent in this. Meaning: even in wine, oil, and honey a mouse certainly spoils; nevertheless one should not compare flies and mosquitoes to a mouse — see there. What he decided in Shulchan Aruch and Rama seems difficult, for those who permit learned from the law of a mouse — that a mouse in all liquids except vinegar and beer spoils; but as Taz's words appear (s.k. 2) agreeing with Rashal that one may not be lenient with a mouse except in three liquids — if so, in flies and ants too one should not be lenient more; perhaps since we find Mordechai brought in Beit Yosef that a fly is lighter than a mouse — even in beer there is no noten ta'am lifgam though we do not rule so — nevertheless in other liquids where several poskim hold a mouse spoils, we rely on them for fly; still requires study.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `Field mouse, etc. It appears from the Mechaber that a field mouse is obvious that it imparts beneficial taste in beer and vinegar since it is served on kings' tables (and possibly therefore even in wine, oil, and the like it does not spoil); but in the Gemara (Avodah Zarah daf 68b) it is proven that even though it is served on kings' tables we are uncertain whether noten ta'am lifgam in mixtures — see there; and even though one can push and reconcile, nevertheless it does not appear so to Rif, Rosh, Tur, Rambam, Ra'avan, and other poskim who did not mention field mouse at all but wrote simply "mouse" — end.`,
    '1#ב': `Or in vinegar if it did not remain inside twenty-four hours, etc. See above siman 105 s.k. 2 from this.`,
    '1#ג': `And specifically in sheretz there is concern, etc. Know that we learn in the last chapter of Avodah Zarah (beginning daf 69): they asked — a mouse fell into vinegar, what? (meaning whether a mouse imparts beneficial taste in vinegar); Rav Hillel told Rav Ashi: there was an incident in Rav Kahana's house and Rav Kahana forbade it; he told him: it crumbled. Rashi explained: it was cut into fine small pieces; and sheretz's measure is like a lentil like its tumah, and we fear a piece of sheretz was absorbed with the vinegar; and so Ra'avan, Mordechai, and Agudah there; and so Or Zarua general rule 32; but Ran wrote on Rashi: wonder why this was needed — in other issurim too there is concern for less than a kezayit, for we hold half a measure is forbidden d'oraisa; and further: strain it and it should be permitted, etc. — end; and to reconcile: certainly in other issurim, since it is noten ta'am lifgam, there is no concern for taste; and also for substance there is none, for it is nullified in majority since it is not recognizable (and see above siman 103 s.k. 3 what is written about dry in dry — and even though not its kind dry we hold sixty, that is because if cooked it would impart taste, which is not so here where it is noten ta'am lifgam); and what is written "strain and permit" — one can say as Tur and Beit Yosef wrote: cut in a way one cannot clarify in a sieve, such as vinegar mixed in thick food; therefore sheretz, since its measure is like a lentil, is not nullified, for it is like beriah, as Or Zarua wrote there; and so appears Tur's view who did not write the law of cut into fine pieces and cannot clarify except here regarding mouse and not other issurim; and no posek omitted to write so for other issurim — this is the Rav's and Bach's view; and from Beit Yosef's words it appears even less than a lentil; and so for other issurim not nullified even in more than sixty whenever it cannot be strained; and so Maharshal Pesachim siman 49; and for now as I wrote; and it seems clear to me Ran did not write so except according to his understanding that it deals where one can strain — but for the law he agrees if one cannot strain it is literally dry in wet, for we hold everywhere we follow majority as written "follow the majority"; know Rashba in Terumot daf 110b wrote like Ran; and in responsum siman 101 wrote: a pot of soup into which a gnat fell — if a limb was cut from it or even whole and crushed and its form lost, it is like other issur and is nullified — end; Beit Yosef brings this in siman 66 — apparently whatever cannot be strained is nullified.`,
    '2#א': `The law is the same. For it certainly spoils; and those who are stringent hold the law of beer and vinegar applies; and so is explained in Beit Yosef and Beit David end of general rule 32; if so, wonder at the Rav's words why he did not permit with one safek in pickling alone, for there is also safek whether it imparts beneficial taste; and if so it is double safek; and it seems this safek whether beneficial or spoiling is not a safek since the Gemara itself was uncertain; or perhaps it is like safek due to lack of knowledge as above siman 98 s.k. 9 — requires study.`,
    '2#ב': `And some are stringent in fat, etc. So is Maharshal Pesachim siman 49 — to forbid in goose fat and other liquids, and he permits only wine, oil, and honey because Rambam wrote these must be spiced — see there.`,
    '2#ג': `Even if there is sixty in the fat. For whatever is poured and touches the mouse becomes forbidden and mixes with the rest of the fat and all becomes forbidden; and even though sometimes the mouse lies at the bottom of the bowl — if so we should permit all fat above the layer on the mouse — one can say it is known when they pour boiling fat everything melts, so the pour always touches the mouse and all becomes forbidden — Beit David general rule 32 law 9 — see there.`,
    '2#ד': `And if they did not heat on it. Meaning boiling, etc., and the fat came before us hard; but if it came soft before us, there is only one safek: perhaps it was there a full day; and safek pickling is forbidden as below siman 105; and if known it was soft when the mouse fell, we forbid from safek perhaps it was pickled in it a full day even if now hard, unless known clearly it was not soft a full day after the mouse entered; and if known soft a full day and later hardened and we do not know if the mouse was in it while soft or not — forbidden; however in all this permitted if there is sixty, unlike heating boiling fat on it — all this Beit David raised there — see there.`,
    '2#ה': `And where the fat is repulsive, etc. Or Zarua wrote general rule 32 law 26: likewise for him if repulsive to him, even if not repulsive to others, forbidden to him even with sixty and a hundred if still repulsive to him — end; and so in Sefer Api Ravrevi daf 57a.`,
    '3#_': `If the heter is greater, etc. Even if there is a kezayit within the time of eating a half-loaf it is permitted — so Rashba wrote; Beit Yosef wrote: even though there are disputants on other noten ta'am lifgam (as explained siman 103 seif 2), it seems for things spoiled in themselves they agree; and there is great reason to distinguish between them — clear; end; and see siman 101.`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#_': `And specifically in sheretz, etc. Beit David wrote the reason: lest something like a lentil remain, which is the measure of its tumah, since from the beginning of its creation it was like a lentil it is called beriah and is not nullified — end; and it seems they brought this to distinguish from what Tur wrote: if cut in a way one cannot clarify and remove it, all is forbidden — clear; for Rama we hold a crushed beriah is nullified, and so in Derishah in the name of Rashal: and even though above siman 101 where issur was crushed it is nullified — sheretz is different, for eating issur is like a lentil like its tumah, and we fear a lentil of sheretz was absorbed; see Rashi in Avodah Zarah daf 69 — end; and he wrote on this that we did not need to distinguish for this, for when cut beriah deals with its kind in its kind and issur is not recognizable within heter; unlike here in thick food where one cannot clarify — nevertheless issur is recognizable among heter — all forbid; only we must distinguish from Beit Yosef: if melted completely, etc., and siman 109 wrote from Terumat HaDeshen: even in not its kind, however nullified in sixty even a kezayit; therefore distinguish other issurim from sheretz — end; and Maharach wrote here like these Rashal words.) I wondered greatly why they thought this — what Tur wrote "if cut," etc. is per the Gemara Avodah Zarah daf 69: mouse fell into vinegar — Rav Hillel told Rav Ashi, there was an incident in Rav Kahana's house and Rav Kahana forbade; he said: it crumbled; Rashi: fine pieces; sheretz issur is like a lentil like its tumah, fear a piece of sheretz was absorbed with vinegar; at first glance it implies he was particular about lentil measure — impossible to say, for half a measure is forbidden d'oraisa, even half a lentil or any amount — we only need a lentil for lashes liability like kezayit of nevelah; and so from Tosafot who challenged: how do we eat bee honey when bee legs are mixed in honey — obvious one who eats honey mixed with bee legs who does not eat lentil measure of legs is not liable; and from their conclusion: what we say limbs have no measure, even less than kezayit from a corpse and less than a lentil from sheretz — learn it deals with less than lentil measure. I also saw Ran in chapter HaSocher challenges Rashi: why lentil — in other issurim too concern for less than kezayit; he challenged further; and Beit David wrote another explanation; and Rashi is not specific to sheratzim but cited the smallest issur most stringent — liable even for lashes on eating; not like Rashal and Rama that sheretz is different; and what Rama wrote in Beit David that if there is like a lentil it is called beriah — I wonder greatly on his holy mouth to say so, for in beriah laws we said explicitly siman 100 regarding ant: if part crushed, beriah importance is nullified; and Beit Yosef siman 101: if part crushed from it, importance nullified; and Beit Yosef siman 101 a fortiori here cut into fine pieces as Tur wrote; and so Or Zarua general rule 25 law 2: like lentil of eight sheratzim, crushed sheretz, etc., even though liable on them, nullified at their measure — end. What Derishah wrote: here issur is recognizable but cannot be clarified — so is true, similar to Beit Yosef siman 115 for Rambam on non-Jewish butter: issur recognizable — the cream — but cannot be clarified, therefore not nullified — see there. It appears Tur here did not come to make sheretz stricter than other issur; he only taught mouse laws in beer and vinegar, and detailed if mixed; what is forbidden when cut is not because of beriah importance but lest a thin piece of sheretz was absorbed in beer or vinegar; Beit Yosef intended this too with half measure forbidden d'oraisa even less than lentil; Tur's explanation: if sheretz melted completely until like liquid of beer and vinegar — measure is in sixty, wet in wet; if cut and not melted — issur substance remains, mixed in liquid, not wet in wet or dry in dry, for all nullification is because unrecognizable — unlike here recognizable but cannot be clarified — therefore sixty does not help here; unlike here where issur was not uprooted and not nullified at all, neither in sixty as wet in wet nor in majority as dry in dry; properly forbidden if one swallows it b'ein — therefore all forbidden here; this is the explanation of "crumbled" in the Gemara; there is no distinction between sheretz and other issurim; the law stated here applies to all issurim if mixed, recognizable, and cannot be clarified — so is clear to me; this hagahah has no place to stand for now, except still to challenge Rashba who wrote on forbidden butter Rambam gave reason because cream does not mix in butter so its minority does not nullify — Rashba challenged as we cite siman 115: even if not mixed it is nullified in minority; if so why is mouse different in beer or vinegar — here too cream in butter is recognizable and dry in wet and should nullify since cannot separate — it seems to me regarding mouse too concern lest one eat the issur piece by itself; granted dry in dry issur becomes heter through majority by Torah decree; not so here issur does not become heter, only taste in butter is permitted because cannot separate issur — that is the basis of heter; but whenever cream is somewhat recognizable, throw it away and do not eat it except as part of butter — therefore permitted; but this does not apply to mouse, lest one eat issur by itself.)`,
    '2#א': `To wine and oil. Rambam gave reason these must be spiced; Rashal chapter 3 siman 48 wrote: from this reason other liquids are forbidden; in honey all agree it spoils as Semag from Tosafot on things mixed in honey; Mordechai chapter Ein Ma'amidin concludes: one should be stringent in all liquids except wine, oil, and honey.`,
    '2#ב': `Perhaps the fat was soft. But if we know certainly the fat was initially soft and there is safek it hardened during pickling — within twenty-four hours from falling — we are stringent, unlike Or Zarua general rule 30 who permits if it came before us hard: we follow time of finding; even if certainly soft at time of issur, perhaps it hardened within twenty-four hours of mouse falling; he brought proof from "all tumah follows time of finding"; Beit David disagrees: with one safek we are stringent.`,
    '2#ג': `And where the fat is repulsive, etc. Meaning it is forbidden — i.e., there is no sixty; so Beit David translated; it seems if there is sixty it is permitted even for lighting; so Beit Yosef in the name of R' Yerucham in Orach Chaim siman 124.`,
    '2#ד': `And where there is loss, etc. This refers to the beginning of hagahah "some are stringent," meaning where there is great loss one may be lenient in all liquids except beer and vinegar.`,
    '3#_': `Repulsive things, etc. Rashal ruled to be stringent; so Maharach concludes to be stringent; but Rama already wrote siman 107 seif 2 not to change the custom practiced to be lenient.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Shulchan Aruch siman 104 seif 1) Mouse fell into beer, etc.; and if cut into fine pieces, etc. — all forbidden for we fear encountering issur substance. Hagahah wrote: and specifically in sheretz we are concerned, etc. — reason explained in Beit David: sheretz from the beginning of creation is like a lentil as in Chagigah daf 11, and that is its measure for tumah and lashes, and so for being beriah and not nullified — see there. I have objections to one of the acharonim in this siman who wrote per Shach that sheretz's measure is like a lentil and is beriah and not nullified — then the question on siman 100 regarding embryo egg where Taz wrote it is beriah is resolved: whence find it — if recognizable from the shell, taste is nullified; if not recognizable, crushed in dish it should nullify, for we hold crushed beriah is nullified as siman 101 seif 6; one can say it deals when crushed in thick food and embryo is sheretz with lentil measure and is beriah — end; his words are surprising, for sheretz like a lentil for beriah is specifically the eight sheratzim with tumah, and when their tumah measure is like a lentil, so eating measure for lashes is like a lentil as in Me'ilah daf 16; since so is their measure for tumah and lashes, so too for not being nullified, for beriah is not nullified because that is its lashes measure, as if the Torah said do not eat them in any amount, as explicit in Ashiri Pesachim and Tosafot and Shita Mekubetzet and Beit Yosef siman 100; but all other sheratzim without tumah — eating measure is kezayit as explicit in Me'ilah, Rambam chapters 2 and 4 of Forbidden Foods and Tum'at Foods, and Tosafot Zevachim 106b and Yevamot 114; if so one cannot say like a lentil of them is beriah and not nullified — there is neither tumah nor lashes; furthermore their beginning of creation is not like a lentil to be considered beriah; furthermore embryo of pure fowl is not sheretz d'oraisa — issur is only rabbinic, as Rambam and Taz siman 88 seif 8 wrote — only impure fowl embryo is d'oraisa as sheretz of fowl; but clear there is no tumah on fowl sheretz and measure is kezayit; see Pesachim 64; therefore his words here are incorrect — requires study.`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 2) If it fell into wine and oil, etc. — one does not need sixty to nullify its emission. Not specifically emission — even if its substance mixed, only that it melted completely, removal suffices as next seif and siman 103; and so must be in what Rosh Pesachim wrote, brought by Beit Yosef s.k. regarding beriah not nullified in a thousand; and what Yerushalmi taught R' Yochanan one mouse in a thousand — not dealing with mouse mixed in heter, etc., until if melted and taste not recognizable, nullified in sixty — end; dealing with mouse mixed in heter that imparts beneficial taste, such as beer and vinegar or fat per Rosh — for in spoiling matter one does not need sixty even when its substance melted, only complete melting or possible straining as Taz seif 1 — clear.`,
    '3#_': `(Hagahah) And some are stringent in fat even if hard before us, etc. Taz wrote Or Zarua permits if it came hard — we follow time of finding; he challenged Beit David and Beit David: with one safek we are stringent; I wonder why not establish fat on presumption of heter; what Rama brought as proof — from tumah following finding — suffices when we do not know it was soft twenty-four hours before; but when we know it was soft twenty-four hours and only do not know if mouse was inside initially — on the contrary we establish from time to time: here found, here it was; but I wrote this is all for kodshim; see Niddah; wonder at Or Zarua who rules cheeses — not like mikveh; so too here; requires study. Even for us who are stringent in both, if within twenty-four hours the vessel with fat was carried place to place and mouse found there, one may permit even if it came soft and we do not know when it fell — we do not establish issur place to place; we say: here it is found, here it was; so in Maimonides Laws of Pesach and responsum Shemen Rokeach — he challenged this; see responsum Panim Me'irrot question 15.`,
  },
};
